Kent Paul Dolan wrote:
> "John H." <Joh...@gmail.com> wrote:
>> Ugh... maybe I'm in the minority here, but I find
>> autocursing a distasteful aspect of the game.
> 
> Well, it's _supposed_ to be distasteful. It's in
> there to make sure that some actions that take risk
> for high reward have ugly consequences when the risk
> goes wrong. Having it be perfectly safe to try on
> unIDed boots in hopes of scoring the speed boots or
> jumping boots that for good players count as "game
> won, just going through the motions now", is a bit
> on the unbalanced side. Having something not _too_
> nasty autocurse helps bring that back into balance.

I dislike autocurse because it makes the boots much less viable an 
option for an early-game character - precisely the characters who could 
benefit from them. If the problem of cursed items being trivial to 
curse-id is too much for you, then I'd recommend making it harder to 
detect curses rather than making more autocursers.

> After all, a loadstone isn't too horrible a problem
> if you shed your alter to be unburdened, and find a
> fountain to take a different kind of risk to uncurse
> it, but a loadstone that didn't autocurse would be
> no danger at all, just pet-test and the luckstone or
> touchstone that are the very valuable look-alike
> alternatives to the loadstone would become freebies.

Loadstones autocurse when you /drop/ them. So pet-test is still 
perfectly safe.

> My parallel distaste is for slowly and carefully
> and very effortfully building up my AC to some "can
> win now" situation, only to run into a part of the
> game seemingly dedicated to destroying AC with Rust
> monsters and Rust traps and Disenchanters and
> destroy armor casters.

Rust monsters are easy - just take off all rustable armor and weild a 
nonrustable weapon. (Most AKs don't include much that rusts anyway - 
DSM, cloak, elven shield or shield of reflection, non-iron boots, shirt, 
leaves only a helmet as a rustable item.)

Disenchanters, on the other hand, are a complete PITA.


